<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modeling with Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A drug is absorbed from your bloodstream at a
        rate proportional to the amount in your
        bloodstream.  That is, if <m>Q</m> is the
        quantity of the drug in your bloodstream, the
        rate of change of <m>Q</m> is proportional
        to <m>Q</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write this as a differential equation using
          <m>k</m> as the constant of proportionality.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dQ}{dt} = -kQ</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the differential equation with the
          initial value <m>Q(0) = Q_0</m>.  If the
          half-life of the drug is 8 hours, find
          the value of <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <m>Q(t) = Q_0 e^{-kt}</m>.  After 8 hours, we know that
          there is half of the original amount, which means that
          <md>
            Q_0 e^{-8k} = \frac{Q_0}{2}
          </md>.
          Therefore
          <md>
            k = -\frac{\ln\left(\frac12\right)}{8} \approx 0.0866
          </md>
          per hour.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose that the drug is administered
          intravenously at the rate of 2 milligrams
          per hour.  Set up a differential equation
          that describes <m>Q(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dQ}{dt} = 2 - kQ</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find any equilibrium solutions and
          determine whether they are stable or
          unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          An equilibrium solution is one where
          <m>dQ/dt = 0</m> so we solve
          <md>
            \frac{dQ}{dt} = 2-kQ = 0
          </md>,
          which shows that the equilibrium solution is
          <md>
            \frac{2}{k} \approx 23.1
          </md>
          mg.  This is a stable
          equilibrium since the right-hand side
          decreases as <m>Q</m> increases through
          <m>Q^*</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the differential equation assuming
          <m>Q(0) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          Separating the equation shows us that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{1}{2-kQ}\frac{dQ}{dt} \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -\frac{1}{k}\ln|2-kQ| \amp = t+C
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          In the usual way, this gives
          <md>
            Q = \frac{2}{k} + Ae^{-kt}
          </md>.
          Since
          <m>Q(0) = 0</m>, we have <m>A = -\frac{2}{k}</m>,
          so
          <md>
            Q(t) = \frac{2}{k}
            \!\left(1 - e^{-kt}\right)
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take for <m>Q</m> to
          reach 90% of the equilibrium?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          We need
          <md>
            Q(t) = \frac2k\left(1-e^{-kt}\right) = 0.9\frac2k
          </md>,
          which means that <m>1 - e^{-kt} = 0.9</m>.  Therefore,
          <m>e^{-kt} = 0.1</m>, so
          <md>
            t = -\frac{\ln(0.1)}{k}
            \approx 26.6
          </md>
          hours.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose you are a doctor and would like
          your patient to have a stable amount of
          50 mg in their bloodstream.  At what rate
          should you administer the drug
          intravenously?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          Let's call <m>r</m> the rate at which the drug is
          administered so that the differential equation becomes
          <md>
            \frac{dQ}{dt} = r-kQ
          </md>.
          The equilibrium is then <m>\frac rk = 50</m> so we need
          <md>
            r=50k\approx 4.33
          </md>
          mg/hr.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The half-life of a particular cough
        suppressant is 3.8 hours.  Assume that the
        rate at which the medicine is absorbed from
        your bloodstream is proportional to the
        amount present, and that you take a 10 mg
        dose.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>Q(t)</m>, the amount of the
          medicine in your bloodstream <m>t</m>
          hours after taking the dose.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          From the half-life, we see that
          <m>k = -\frac{\ln(0.5)}{3.8}</m>.  With
          <m>Q(0) = 10</m>:
          <md>
            Q(t) = 10\,e^{-kt}
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long until there is only 1 mg in
          your bloodstream?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          Setting <m>10\,e^{-kt} = 1</m>:
          <m>e^{-kt} = \frac{1}{10}</m>, so
          <md>
            t = -\frac{\ln(0.1)}{k}
            \approx 12.6
          </md>
          hours.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        Suppose that you take another 10mg does at this point.  How
        much is left in your bloodstream 4 hours later?
      </statement>
      <solution component="34">
        <p>
          Just after you take the next 10mg dose, you have 11mg in
          your bloodstream so
          <md>
            Q(t)=11e^{-kt}
          </md>.
          Four hours later, you have
          <md>
            Q(4) = 11e^{-4k} = \approx 5.3
          </md>
          mg.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
